PLACES=[
N('black-jack-battlefield-ks','Black Jack Battlefield','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','fort','traces',38.76400,-95.13082,'site','public','walk','','1856',
  'On June 2, 1856, John Brown and a free-state band attacked the camp of the proslavery militia of Henry Clay Pate at Black Jack Creek, about three miles east of present Baldwin City; after about three hours of fighting Pate surrendered and nobody was killed. It is counted as one of the clashes of "Bleeding Kansas" before the Civil War. Robert Hall Pearson, who fought with Brown, later bought the ground and built the farmhouse that stands above the battlefield (the dates disagree: a Clio entry drawn from the Kansas register gives the purchase as 1860 and the house as about 1890; a 2006 Journal-World article puts the purchase in the 1880s). The battlefield was made a National Historic Landmark in 2012.',
  'The 2004 Register form takes in three pieces: Black Jack Park, a two-acre roadside park with a 1969 log cabin and the creek, and the Ivan Boyd Prairie with its trail ruts, together a 20-acre tract east of 2000 Road and south of US 56; and the quarter-acre Robert Hall Pearson Memorial Park on the west side of 2000 Road at North 175 Road, given to the county in 1970, with a granite battle marker, a 1913 Grand Army marker and a sign naming 48 men who fought. Wooded ravines run west of the road. The Pearson farmhouse stands on the hill, and a self-guided tour with numbered stops starts at the memorial park. Not checked on the ground.',
  'Where John Brown fought his first real battle, three years before Harpers Ferry.',
  'Black Jack Battlefield and Nature Park, 163 E 2000 Road, Wellsville, just south of US 56 about three miles east of Baldwin City. The county lists it among its parks and says it is open daily from dawn to dusk (the federal public-land map marks the trust\'s ground as closed; the county page says open); a nonprofit trust bought the Pearson farm in 2003 and runs the park. The farmhouse is open only at times (not checked on the trust\'s own page, which would not open here). Park on the half-circle drive of Black Jack Park, off US 56 at 2000 Road, or as the park signs say; the 2025 Park Day listing says there are no toilets on site.',
  [W('Battle of Black Jack'),['National Register form, 2004',NR%'04000365'],['Douglas County: Black Jack Battlefield and Nature Park','https://dgcoks.gov/locations/county-parks/black-jack-nature-park'],['Clio: Robert Hall Pearson house','https://theclio.com/entry/106822'],['Lawrence Journal-World, 2006','https://www2.ljworld.com/news/2006/may/25/park_site_will_become_nature_preserve/']],
  conf='mixed',th=[38.76740,-95.12892],thn='Black Jack Park drive, US 56 at 2000 Road'),
N('black-jack-ruts-ivan-boyd-prairie','Black Jack Ruts (Ivan Boyd Prairie)','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','landmark','traces',38.7669,-95.1294,'site','public','walk','','1821–1870s',
  'Wagons on the Santa Fe Trail cut a set of branching ruts into this hillside east of Baldwin City. Douglas County bought the tract of native prairie in 1967 and named it for Ivan Boyd, a Baker University biology professor who pushed for it. The Register form describes a six-and-a-half-acre site with four rutted segments that branch like tributaries, two limestone markers set in 1965 and a modern wooden sign.',
  'Swales running across the prairie, easiest to read when the grass is short or snow lies in them. A gravel trail with interpretive signs crosses the prairie to the ruts; there are also a log cabin (built in 1971 by the county page, 1969 by the Register form) and roadside markers by the drive.',
  'The best ruts on the Santa Fe Trail in eastern Kansas, a short walk from the lot.',
  'Owned by Douglas County and kept by the Douglas County chapter of the Santa Fe Trail Historical Society (county park page). On the south side of US 56 about three miles east of Baldwin City, on the east side of 2000 Road; the Pearson Memorial Park and the battlefield park are across 2000 Road. The county page gives no hours. Stay on the trail: the prairie is the point of the place.',
  [['National Register form (Santa Fe Trail, Douglas County segments)',NR%'02001262'],['Douglas County: Ivan Boyd Prairie Preserve','https://dgcoks.gov/locations/county-parks/ivan-boyd-prairie-preserve'],['HMdb: Ivan L. Boyd Memorial Prairie Preserve','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=63702'],['Clio: Santa Fe Trail Marker #6 and the Ivan Boyd Prairie','https://theclio.com/entry/189284']],
  th=[38.76740,-95.12892],thn='Black Jack Park drive, US 56 at 2000 Road'),
N('trail-park-baldwin-city','Trail Park and the Santa Fe Trail road','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','landmark','traces',38.7910,-95.1867,'site','public','drive','','1907',
  'North 400 Road on the north edge of Baldwin City runs on the line of the Santa Fe Trail, which maps of 1857 already show folded into the local roads. In 1907 Isaiah and Jennie Stickle gave Baker University a small parcel beside the angled road to mark the trail, and the Kansas DAR set one of its granite trail markers here, one of 93 they placed across Kansas between 1906 and 1914. The marker\'s original bronze plaque was stolen in 1967 and replaced in marble.',
  'A small triangle of grass with the granite DAR marker beside the old angled road. The Register form says remnants of the trail can be seen in private fields starting about two-thirds of a mile northwest of the marker; those fields are not public. The Palmyra Well marker, for a well dug in 1857 for the town that became Baldwin City, is about half a mile away on Washington Street; whether the well itself can be seen was not checked.',
  'A county road that is still the Santa Fe Trail, with the 1907 marker that says so.',
  'The parcel was given to Baker University in 1907 (Kansas Historical Society); its present owner was not checked. A roadside park at the northwest corner of East 1700 Road (North 6th Street) and North 400 Road, about three-quarters of a mile north of US 56. Park on the road shoulder. Look at the trail traces in the fields from the road only.',
  [['National Register form',NR%'13000486'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%11],['HMdb: Trail Park','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=20075'],['HMdb: Palmyra Well','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=91201']]),
N('willow-springs-santa-fe-trail-swale','Willow Springs trail swale and DAR marker','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','landmark','traces',38.818762,-95.297897,'exact','view','drive','','1821–1866',
  'Willow Springs was a well-known watering stop on the eastern part of the Santa Fe Trail, a little over six miles northwest of Baldwin City; the town that grew there is gone. A quarter-acre listed on the National Register in 2018 holds a visible trail swale and one of the granite markers the Kansas DAR set along the trail early in the 1900s.',
  'The granite DAR marker ("Santa Fe Trail 1822–1872", set in 1906) stands on a rubble base on the west side of East 1100 Road at North 550 Road. The listed swale runs east-west in pasture on the north side of North 550 Road, from about 200 to 350 m east of the marker. The form records at least two more swales south of the road and a group of six in a pasture farther east, all on land whose owners did not agree to list them. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A trail stop on the way to Santa Fe, reduced to a dip in a field and a stone.',
  'The swale is on private pasture (Register form): look from North 550 Road only. The marker belongs to the Kansas Society DAR and stands in the road right-of-way. There is no lot; pull well off the gravel road.',
  [['National Register form, 2018',NR%'100002964'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%13]]),
N('old-castle-hall-baker-university','Old Castle Hall, Baker University','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',38.77722,-95.18444,'exact','view','walk','','1857–58',
  'The first building of Baker University and the first college building in Kansas, built of rubble stone in 1857 and 1858; classes were held in it from November 1858 until 1871, and afterward it served as a mill and a dormitory. It was restored in the 1950s and is a museum of the university, the Methodists and early Baldwin City. Beside it stands the frame post office of Palmyra, the trail town that Baldwin City replaced: built in 1857, it served the Santa Fe Trail from 1857 to 1862, is the only building left from Palmyra, and was moved here in the 1970s.',
  'A three-story stone building with a red roof, the moved Palmyra post office on one side and a replica log cabin, the Kibbee Cabin, on the other.',
  'The oldest college building in Kansas, and the last building of the town that was here before it.',
  'On the Baker University campus at 511 Fifth Street. Baker\'s page says the museum is open by appointment (archives, 785-594-8380). The outside can be seen from the street at any time.',
  [W('Old Castle Hall'),['Baker University: Old Castle Museum','https://bakeru.edu/old-castle'],['Clio: Baldwin City driving tour','https://theclio.com/tour/1418/5'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary (Palmyra Post Office)',KSNR%9]],
  th=[38.77640,-95.18428],thn='Lot on Fifth Street'),
N('baldwin-city-santa-fe-depot','Santa Fe depot, Baldwin City','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','rail','standing',38.77472,-95.20111,'exact','view','drive','stations','1907',
  'The Santa Fe built this brick depot on its Lawrence-to-Ottawa line in 1906 and 1907 to replace a wooden one. Passenger trains stopped in the mid-1950s and freight about a decade later. From 1987 the Midland Railway ran excursion trains from here over the old line toward Ottawa; the depot belongs to the city and was leased to the local Santa Fe Trail association, which evicted the railway\'s for-profit arm in March 2020 for unpaid rent, stopping its rides. A new owner of the Midland planned to bring trains back in 2022, and in December 2025 the town\'s Railfest offered train rides from the depot.',
  'The 1907 depot on High Street with track beside it.',
  'A trail town\'s depot that outlived its passenger trains by seventy years and still sends out the odd excursion.',
  'At 1515 High Street. The building can be seen from the street; whether and when it is open, and the current train schedule, were not checked. Do not walk on the track.',
  [W('Baldwin City station'),W('Midland Railway (Kansas)'),['Lawrence Journal-World, March 2020','https://www2.ljworld.com/news/general-news/2020/mar/08/baldwin-city-southern-evicted-from-depot-ends-tourist-rides/'],['KOFO, December 2025','https://kofo.com/?p=11715']]),
N('constitution-hall-lecompton','Constitution Hall, Lecompton','Lecompton, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.04539,-95.39468,'exact','public','walk','','1857',
  'Lecompton was the proslavery territorial capital of Kansas from 1855 to 1861. Sheriff Samuel J. Jones put up this two-story frame building and rented it to the territorial government: in 1857 the federal land office filled the ground floor, and upstairs met the court, the legislature and the convention that wrote the proslavery Lecompton Constitution. The Kansas Historical Society runs it as a state historic site; it became a National Historic Landmark in 1974.',
  'The plain white frame hall on Elmore Street, with exhibits on both floors.',
  'The building where Kansas nearly became a slave state.',
  'Constitution Hall State Historic Site, 319 Elmore Street. Kansas Historical Society page: open year round, 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. Wednesday to Saturday and 1 to 5 p.m. Sunday, closed state holidays, free admission. Accessible parking behind the hall at 330 Isacks Street; the second floor is not accessible.',
  [W('Constitution Hall (Lecompton, Kansas)'),W('Lecompton, Kansas'),['Kansas Historical Society: plan your visit','https://www.kansashistory.gov/p/constitution-hall-plan-your-visit/15523']]),
N('lane-university-capitol-foundation-lecompton','Lane University on the unfinished capitol foundation','Lecompton, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',39.04494,-95.39194,'exact','public','walk','','1865–1933',
  'Lecompton laid the foundations of a territorial capitol but only began the building before Kansas entered the Union as a free state. In 1865 the state gave 13 acres, foundation included, to the new Lane University, and in 1882 the university put up a stone building on the south half of the old foundation. The parents of Dwight Eisenhower met there as students and married in its chapel in 1885. The school merged into Campbell College in 1902 and the building is now the Territorial Capital Museum, kept by the Lecompton Historical Society.',
  'The 1882 stone college building, standing on the capitol foundation; the museum tells the story of the capitol that was never finished. How much of the old foundation can be seen outside was not checked.',
  'A college built on the footings of a proslavery capitol.',
  'Territorial Capital Museum, 640 East Woodson Avenue, two blocks from Constitution Hall. The town\'s website gives Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. and Sunday 1 to 5 p.m. with a suggested donation; an older listing gives 11 to 4. Neither shows a date, so call ahead (785-887-6148).',
  [W('Lane University'),['Lecompton: Lane University and the Territorial Capital Museum','https://lecomptonkansas.com/lane-university/'],['Lecompton: hours','https://lecomptonkansas.com/tag/museum']],
  conf='mixed'),
N('bloomington-clinton-lake','Bloomington: Kansas Avenue and the Steele house steps','Clinton Lake, Kansas','ks','town','traces',38.91004,-95.37065,'site','public','walk','drowned','1850s–1970s',
  'Bloomington grew up in the Wakarusa valley in territorial days; by 1865 most of its first settlers had left, and Black families who moved in kept its church and school going, the school taking white and Black pupils together for a time and closing in 1942. The Corps of Engineers bought the valley for Clinton Lake, finished in 1975, and Bloomington, Belvoir, Sigel and Richland were cleared or drowned. James C. Steele, who arrived in 1857, built a two-story brick house here from bricks he fired on the river; it is said to have been a stop on the Underground Railroad.',
  'On the grounds of the Wakarusa River Valley Heritage Museum a long stretch of Kansas Avenue, the old road to Lawrence, survives as a roadbed lined with tall walnut trees, with a sign. All that remains of the Steele house is its front porch steps; its windows and bricks are reused at the museum. A stone smokehouse of 1892 from Bloomington stands on the grounds. The schoolhouse site is under the beach and has nothing left.',
  'A town the lake took, where the main street still runs into the water.',
  'Bloomington Park on the west side of Clinton Lake, on Corps of Engineers land (Clinton Recreation Area). The museum is at the park entrance; its hours and the park\'s day-use rules were not checked.',
  [W('Clinton Lake (Kansas)'),['Clio: Kansas Avenue','https://theclio.com/entry/167500'],['Clio: J.C. Steele house','https://theclio.com/entry/167197'],['Clio: Bloomington walking tour','https://theclio.com/tour/2461'],['Clio: Bloomington schoolhouse','https://theclio.com/entry/167601']],
  th=[38.90788,-95.36852],thn='Lot on North 1190 Road, Bloomington Park'),
N('lone-star-lake-ccc-camp','Lone Star Lake CCC camp','Lone Star, Kansas','ks','civic','ruins',38.83767,-95.37566,'area','public','drive','','1934–1939',
  'The Civilian Conservation Corps began a dam on Washington Creek in 1934 for the state fish and game commission; the county took the project over in 1937 and the lake was finished in 1939. The CCC camp that housed the men had 26 buildings. The camp remnants and the dam were added to the National Register in 2023.',
  'Only stone pieces of the camp are left in the park campground: a stone water tower, four stone fireplaces, a stone firepit and a stone staircase (Kansas Historical Society summary). The CCC dam with its guardrails closes the north end of the lake, with a road along its top (aerial). Not checked on the ground.',
  'A Depression work camp reduced to its chimneys and a water tower beside the lake its men built.',
  'Lone Star Lake Park, 665 East 665 Road, owned and run by Douglas County. Camping April to mid-October by permit, first come first served; quiet hours 10 p.m. to 6 a.m. The county page gives no day-use hours.',
  [W('Lone Star Lake'),['Douglas County: Lone Star Lake Park','https://dgcoks.gov/locations/county-parks/lone-star-lake-park'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%7],['National Register form (dam)','https://www.kansashistory.gov/resource/national_register/nominationsNRDB/DouglasCo_LoneStarLakeDam_listed_10172023_compres.pdf']],
  th=[38.83854,-95.37549],thn='Parking along the shore road by the campground (a lot in OpenStreetMap)'),
N('chicken-creek-arch-lone-star','Chicken Creek stone arch','Lone Star, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.85208,-95.34395,'site','public','drive','','1913',
  'A single stone arch over Chicken Creek, built about 1913 by B. F. Metsker and Ike Babb with locally quarried limestone. Its 1990 Register form calls it one of two masonry arch bridges of its size left in Douglas County, after the county replaced many old bridges in the 1970s.',
  'A semicircular arch about 14 feet across and 11 feet above the water, with stone parapets on top. On the aerial the county road now curves east of an old gravel stretch of road that crosses the creek; the arch should be on that old stretch, which the county keeps as a small park. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A country stone arch the county turned into a park.',
  'Chicken Creek Arch Park, 780 E 850 Road, half a mile east and a mile and a quarter south of Lone Star (Douglas County). The county page gives no hours.',
  [['National Register form',NR%'90000298'],['Douglas County: Chicken Creek Arch Park','https://www.dgcoks.gov/locations/county-parks/chicken-creek-arch-park']],
  th=[38.85162,-95.34386],thn='Pull-off at Chicken Creek Arch Park'),
N('upper-wakarusa-river-crossing','Upper Wakarusa River Crossing','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','traces',38.9098,-95.2369,'site','ask','walk','','1840s–1860',
  'Oregon- and California-bound wagons forded the Wakarusa River here, south of present Lawrence, on the first days of the trip west; the route later became the road from Belton, Missouri, to Lawrence. Two cutdowns where wagons came down the north bank survive, about 320 feet apart, each 20 to 30 feet wide and 60 feet long; nothing is left on the south bank. It was listed on the National Register in 2013.',
  'Two notches in the north bank of the river, in woods south of the Baker Wetlands. Not checked on the ground.',
  'Where the Oregon Trail got its feet wet on day two.',
  'Private land (Register form, 2013): ask the owner first. The form gives the address 1180 E 1400 Road. No public way to the river bank was found.',
  [['National Register form',NR%'13001039'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%12]]),
N('bowersock-dam-lawrence','Bowersock Dam and powerhouse','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',38.9753,-95.2353,'site','view','walk','','1874–now',
  'A stone-and-timber dam was built across the Kansas River at Lawrence in 1874 to drive the town\'s mills by cable and water wheel; it failed its first builder, Orlando Darling, and passed to James Gower and his son-in-law J. D. Bowersock, whose company still runs it. It has made electricity since 1905, and with a second powerhouse on the north bank it is the only hydroelectric plant in Kansas. Its pool also feeds one of the city\'s water plants.',
  'The low dam across the river beside the Massachusetts Street bridge, with the old powerhouse on the south bank and the newer one on the north.',
  'The dam that made Lawrence an industrial town, still making power after 150 years.',
  'Owned by the Bowersock Mills and Power Company. Seen from the Massachusetts Street bridge and the riverfront; the company says it welcomes visitors to tour the plant (call to arrange). Stay off the dam and out of the water below it.',
  [W('Bowersock Dam'),['Bowersock Mills and Power: about','https://bowersockpower.com/about/'],['Bowersock Mills and Power: history','https://bowersockpower.com/about/our-history/']],
  th=[38.97700,-95.23493],thn='Lot on Elm Street, North Lawrence'),
N('coal-creek-library-vinland','Coal Creek Library','Vinland, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.8401,-95.1826,'exact','view','drive','','1859; building 1900',
  'Twenty-two neighbors in the Coal Creek valley formed a library association on November 22, 1859, and kept its books in their homes for 41 years. In May 1900 they built this one-room frame building, like a country schoolhouse with a porch. It is counted as the oldest library in Kansas, and was raised, braced and set on a new foundation in a restoration finished in 2023.',
  'A one-room white frame building with a porch, still holding many of the first books, beside Vinland\'s Grange Hall and Presbyterian church.',
  'A frontier book club that never closed.',
  'At 696 East 1719 Road in Vinland. Opening times were not checked (it opens for limited hours, such as Sunday afternoons in 2023). See it from the road otherwise.',
  [W('Vinland, Kansas'),['Lawrence Times, October 2023','https://lawrencekstimes.com/2023/10/19/harper-coal-creek-library-oped/']]),
]
